**Laziness proof — intermediate ops do nothing until terminal:**

```java
// This entire chain does ZERO work:
Stream<Trade> pipeline = trades.stream()
        .filter(t -> { System.out.println("filter: " + t);
        return t.isSettled(); })
        .map(t -> { System.out.println("map: " + t);
        return t.getAmount(); })
    .sorted();
// Console output: NOTHING! No filter, no map, no sort executed!

// NOW add a terminal:
List<BigDecimal> result = pipeline.toList();
// Console output: filter, map, sort all execute NOW!
```

**Short-circuiting operations — two categories:**

Short-circuiting INTERMEDIATE: `limit(n)`, `takeWhile(Predicate)` — truncate the stream.

Short-circuiting TERMINAL: `findFirst()`, `findAny()`, `anyMatch()`, `allMatch()`, `noneMatch()` — stop processing entirely.

```java
// findFirst — stops after first match:
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .findFirst();  // pulls elements until one passes filter → STOPS

// anyMatch — stops on first true:
boolean hasFraud = trades.stream()
    .anyMatch(t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(limit) > 0);
// First trade exceeding limit → returns true, STOPS

// allMatch — stops on first false:
boolean allSettled = trades.stream()
    .allMatch(Trade::isSettled);
// First unsettled trade → returns false, STOPS

// limit — truncates after n elements:
trades.stream()
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)
    .limit(3)       // after 3 settled trades pass → pipeline stops
    .toList();
```

**Fusion — single pass vs multi-pass:**

```java
// WITHOUT fusion (hypothetical — what people THINK happens):
// Pass 1: filter ALL trades → intermediateList1
// Pass 2: map ALL remaining → intermediateList2
// Pass 3: sorted ALL → intermediateList3
// Pass 4: collect → result

// WITH fusion (what ACTUALLY happens):
// For each element from source:
//   filter → (if passes) → map → accumulate into result
// ONE element through ALL stateless ops before next element pulled
// No intermediate collections created!
```

**Combining laziness + short-circuit + fusion — the power:**

```java
// 10 MILLION trades, but we only need first 3 settled:
trades.stream()                   // 10M elements
    .filter(Trade::isSettled)      // lazy — checks one at a time
    .map(Trade::getAmount)         // lazy — transforms one at a time
    .limit(3)                      // short-circuit: stop after 3!
    .toList();
// If first 5 trades include 3 settled ones:
// → Only 5 elements pulled from source
// → 9,999,995 elements NEVER touched!
// → filter+map fused: each element goes through both in one step
```

